/* NOSTRAVECTOR · residents' dialogue pack (part 4: real life)
   When you tell a resident you're stressed in real life, or ask for job advice, they step out of character (villains too).
   They are not therapists or careers advisers: they listen, suggest simple, well-known techniques, and point you to real
   people and services. Anything urgent goes straight to emergency and crisis contacts. */
window.NV_RES_LINES = Object.assign(window.NV_RES_LINES || {}, {
care: {
  intro: ['Hey. Stepping out of the game for a second. I’m listening.', 'Out of character for a moment: thank you for telling me. That takes something.', 'Forget the game for a minute. How are you, really?', 'Dropping the act. Real life can be heavy, and you don’t have to carry it alone.', 'I’m just a character in a game, not a therapist, but I can listen and share a few things that help people.', 'Ok, no roleplay. You matter more than any quest. Let’s talk.', 'I’m glad you said something. Want to tell me what’s going on?', 'Pausing the villain stuff. Seriously, I’m here. What’s up?'],
  topics: {
    work: { label: 'Work', validate: ['Work stress can follow you home and sit on your chest. That’s real.', 'Feeling stretched thin at work is exhausting, and it’s not a personal failing.', 'A lot of people feel like they’re never doing enough at work. You’re not alone in that.', 'It makes sense to feel worn down if work keeps asking for more than you have.', 'Work pressure can make everything else feel harder too.', 'It sounds like work has been taking a lot out of you.'], ideas: ['Could you write down the three things that actually have to happen this week? Just three.', 'If you can, take a proper break today, away from the screen, even ten minutes.', 'Is there one task you could ask for help with, or push back on?', 'Try ending the day by writing tomorrow’s first small step. It helps your brain let go.', 'It might help to talk to your manager about workload, if that feels safe.', 'Notice if you’re skipping meals or sleep for work. Those come first.', 'Some workplaces have an employee assistance programme with free, confidential support. Worth checking.', 'Setting one boundary, like no emails after a certain time, can make a real difference.'] },
    study: { label: 'School or exams', validate: ['Exams and deadlines can make everything feel like it’s riding on one moment. It isn’t, even if it feels that way.', 'Studying under pressure is tiring, and stress is a normal response to it.', 'Lots of people feel behind. Feeling behind doesn’t mean you are.', 'It’s hard to focus when you’re anxious. That’s your body, not a lack of effort.', 'School can be a lot, especially with everything else going on.', 'Grades matter, but they don’t measure your worth as a person.'], ideas: ['Try studying in short blocks, like 25 minutes on, 5 off. Small and repeatable beats long and miserable.', 'Pick one topic for today instead of everything at once.', 'Tell a teacher, tutor or lecturer you’re struggling. They’d usually rather know early.', 'Sleep helps memory more than late-night cramming does.', 'Study with a friend, even on a call, so it feels less lonely.', 'Write down what you’re worried will happen, then what you’d do if it did. It often shrinks.', 'Most schools and universities have wellbeing or counselling services. They’re there for exactly this.', 'Move your body a little between sessions. A short walk resets your head.'] },
    relationships: { label: 'Relationships or family', validate: ['Things with people we care about can hurt the most. That makes sense.', 'Conflict with someone close can make it hard to think about anything else.', 'It’s ok to feel hurt, angry and still care about someone, all at once.', 'Family stuff is complicated. Your feelings about it are valid.', 'Breakups and fallings-out can feel like grief. Because they kind of are.', 'You deserve relationships where you feel safe and respected.'], ideas: ['Is there someone outside the situation you trust who you could talk to?', 'If a conversation is needed, it can help to write down what you want to say first.', 'Try "I feel... when... because..." instead of "you always". It lands softer.', 'It’s ok to take space before responding to something that upset you.', 'Look after the basics while it’s hard: food, sleep, a bit of fresh air.', 'If you ever feel unsafe with someone, that’s serious. Please reach out to people or services who can help keep you safe.', 'Relationship counselling or family mediation exists for situations like this, if it’s an option.', 'You can’t control how others act, only how you look after yourself through it.'] },
    money: { label: 'Money', validate: ['Money worries can keep you up at night. That’s a really common, really heavy stress.', 'Not having enough is stressful, and it’s not a character flaw.', 'Money stress affects sleep, mood, everything. No wonder you’re feeling it.', 'Lots of people are struggling with costs right now. You’re not the only one.', 'It’s hard to think clearly when you’re worried about bills.', 'Asking for help with money can feel embarrassing. It shouldn’t, and it’s smart.'], ideas: ['Writing down exactly what comes in and goes out can make it feel less shapeless, even if the numbers are hard.', 'Deal with the most urgent bill first: housing and energy usually come before others.', 'Many places have free, confidential debt and money advice services. Worth searching for one near you.', 'If you owe money, contacting the company early to ask for a payment plan often works better than waiting.', 'Check if you’re entitled to any support or benefits you’re not claiming.', 'Be wary of quick loans with high interest. They can make things worse.', 'Pick one small money task today, like opening that letter. Just one.', 'Talk to someone you trust. Money worries shrink a little when they’re shared.'] },
    health: { label: 'Health', validate: ['Worrying about your health, or living with something hard, is exhausting.', 'It’s ok to be scared about health stuff. That’s human.', 'Being unwell can make everything else feel twice as hard.', 'Waiting for results or answers is one of the hardest kinds of stress.', 'You don’t have to be brave all the time about it.', 'Your feelings about your health make sense.'], ideas: ['If something is worrying you, please talk to a doctor or health service. They’d rather see you early.', 'Write your questions down before appointments so you don’t forget them.', 'Try not to spiral on internet searches. A real professional knows your situation.', 'Bring someone you trust to appointments if that would help.', 'Be gentle with what you expect from yourself while you’re unwell.', 'Your mental health is health too. Doctors can help with stress, low mood and anxiety.', 'Small routines, like a regular bedtime, can steady things when health feels uncertain.', 'Support groups for specific conditions can help you feel less alone.'] },
    lonely: { label: 'Feeling lonely', validate: ['Loneliness hurts. It’s one of the most human feelings there is.', 'You can feel lonely even surrounded by people. That’s real too.', 'It takes courage to say you feel alone.', 'Feeling lonely doesn’t mean something is wrong with you.', 'Lots of people feel this way and hide it. You’re not the only one.', 'I’m glad you’re talking to me, even if I’m just a game character.'], ideas: ['Is there one person you could send a short message to today? Even just "hey, thinking of you".', 'Groups around a hobby, a class or volunteering are easier ways to meet people than starting from nothing.', 'Online communities for games you like can be a gentle start.', 'Getting outside, even to a café or park, can take the edge off.', 'Talking services and befriending schemes exist for exactly this, in many places.', 'Be patient with yourself. Connection usually builds slowly.', 'Pets, plants, routines: small things to care for can help.', 'If loneliness is turning into hopelessness, please talk to a doctor or a support line.'] },
    everything: { label: 'Everything at once', validate: ['When it’s everything at once, it’s hard to even know where to start. That’s ok.', 'Feeling overwhelmed is your mind telling you it’s carrying too much, not that you’re weak.', 'It makes sense to feel flattened when life piles on.', 'You don’t have to fix it all today.', 'Overwhelm can make small tasks feel huge. That’s normal.', 'Thank you for telling me. Saying it out loud is a first step.'], ideas: ['Let’s shrink it: what’s one thing that would make the next hour a tiny bit easier?', 'Try a brain dump: write every worry down, then circle just one to look at.', 'Drink some water, eat something, and step outside if you can. Basics first.', 'It’s ok to say no to anything that isn’t essential right now.', 'Tell one person you trust that you’re overwhelmed. You don’t need a plan to do that.', 'Sleep on big decisions if you can.', 'A doctor or counsellor can help if overwhelm keeps coming back.', 'Be as kind to yourself as you’d be to a friend in your place.'] },
    other: { label: 'Something else', validate: ['Whatever it is, it matters if it’s weighing on you.', 'You don’t have to explain it perfectly. It’s ok to just say it’s hard.', 'Thanks for trusting me with it.', 'It sounds like you’re carrying something heavy.', 'Your feelings make sense, even if the situation is complicated.', 'I’m here, for whatever a game character is worth.'], ideas: ['Would talking to someone you trust about it help?', 'Writing it down can help untangle it.', 'Try to look after the basics today: food, water, rest.', 'If it’s been building for a while, a doctor or counsellor could really help.', 'One small kind thing for yourself today. You deserve it.', 'Take a breath with me first. There’s a breathing exercise if you want it.', 'Sometimes stepping away for a walk gives you a different angle on things.', 'You don’t have to solve it all now.'] }
  },
  tools: {
    breathe: ['Let’s try box breathing. Sit comfortably.', 'Breathe in slowly through your nose for four... two... three... four.', 'Hold it gently for four.', 'Breathe out through your mouth for four.', 'Hold for four.', 'Again: in for four... hold... out for four... hold.', 'One more round, as slow as feels ok.', 'Notice your shoulders. Let them drop a little.', 'That’s it. You can do this any time, even in a queue or before a meeting.'],
    ground: ['Let’s try grounding. Look around wherever you are.', 'Name five things you can see.', 'Four things you can touch. Notice how they feel.', 'Three things you can hear.', 'Two things you can smell, or like the smell of.', 'One thing you can taste, or one good thing about right now.', 'You’re here, you’re ok in this moment. That’s what grounding is for.'],
    small: ['Drink a glass of water.', 'Stand up and stretch for a minute.', 'Open a window or step outside.', 'Put on a song you love.', 'Text someone you like.', 'Tidy one tiny space, like your desk.', 'Have a snack.', 'Close the game for a bit and come back later. I’ll still be here.', 'Write down one thing you got through today.', 'Wash your face or have a shower.'],
    kind: ['You’re doing the best you can with what you have.', 'Rest isn’t something you have to earn.', 'Struggling doesn’t make you a burden.', 'You’ve survived every hard day so far.', 'It’s ok to not be ok.', 'Progress doesn’t have to be big to count.', 'You deserve the same patience you give others.', 'Your worth isn’t measured by productivity.', 'Hard feelings pass, even when they feel permanent.', 'Asking for help is a strength.'],
    sleep: ['Try to keep the same wake-up time, even after a bad night.', 'Screens off a little before bed helps your brain wind down.', 'If you can’t sleep, get up for a bit and do something calm, then try again.', 'Keep the room cool and dark if you can.', 'Writing worries down before bed can stop them circling.', 'Go easy on caffeine after lunch.'],
    worry: ['Try "worry time": pick fifteen minutes later today to think about worries.', 'When a worry pops up before then, jot it down and tell it "later".', 'In worry time, go through the list: can I do anything about this?', 'If yes, pick one small step. If no, practise letting it sit without solving it.', 'Many people find their worries feel smaller by the time worry time comes.', 'It takes practice. Be patient with it.']
  },
  checkin: ['How are you feeling now, compared to a few minutes ago?', 'Any better, the same, or worse?', 'Want to try something else, or is that enough for now?', 'Checking in: how’s your breathing?', 'Is there anything else on your mind?', 'Do you want to keep talking, or take a break?'],
  better: ['I’m really glad. Come back any time you need a minute.', 'Good. Remember the breathing works anywhere.', 'Proud of you for taking the time.', 'That’s great. Be gentle with yourself for the rest of the day.', 'Glad it helped a little. Little counts.', 'Ok. I’ll go back to being a character now. Look after yourself.'],
  same: ['That’s ok. Some days are just hard, and talking doesn’t fix everything.', 'If it keeps feeling like this, please talk to a doctor, counsellor or someone you trust. You deserve real support.', 'It might help to reach out to a friend or family member today, even briefly.', 'You don’t have to feel better right away. Thank you for trying.', 'If things get worse, please use the "I’m not safe" option or contact a crisis line. You matter.', 'I’m only a game character, but real people want to help. Please let them.'],
  support: ['Talking to someone you trust is one of the most helpful things you can do.', 'A doctor or GP can help with stress, anxiety and low mood, and can point you to talking therapies.', 'Counsellors and therapists are trained for exactly this.', 'If you’re at school, college or university, their wellbeing service is free.', 'Some workplaces offer free confidential support lines.', 'Helplines are there to listen too, even if it’s not an emergency.', 'Crisis lines are free and confidential, and you don\u2019t need to be in an emergency to call one.', 'Reaching out is brave, not weak.'],
  safety: ['Thank you for telling me. Your safety matters more than anything in this game.', 'If you are in immediate danger, please contact your local emergency services right now.', 'You can also speak to a crisis line. They\u2019re free, confidential, and there to listen any time, day or night.', 'If you\u2019re not sure how to reach one, a doctor, a pharmacist, or someone you trust can help you find it.', 'If you can, tell someone near you how you\u2019re feeling right now. You don\u2019t have to go through this alone.', 'I\u2019m a game character and can\u2019t help the way a person can. Please reach out to one of them.', 'You deserve support, and people want to give it.']
},
jobs: {
  intro: ['Out of character: happy to share some general job tips. I’m not a careers adviser, but here’s what tends to help.', 'Real-world job stuff? Sure. Pausing the roleplay.', 'Let’s talk real jobs. These are general tips, so adapt them to your situation.', 'Job hunting and work life can be stressful. Let’s break it down.', 'Dropping character. What do you want help with?'],
  topics: {
    search: { label: 'Finding a job', tips: ['Treat the search like a project: a few focused hours a day beats endless scrolling.', 'Tailor each application. Ten good ones beat fifty copy-paste ones.', 'Tell people you’re looking. Lots of jobs are found through people you already know.', 'Set up alerts on job sites so you’re not checking constantly.', 'Look at company websites directly, not just job boards.', 'Keep a simple list of where you’ve applied and when, so you can follow up.', 'Following up politely after a week or two is normal and can help.', 'Rejections are part of the process, not a verdict on you.', 'Volunteering, short courses or projects can fill gaps and build skills.', 'Look for transferable skills from things you’ve already done, including hobbies.', 'Recruitment agencies can help in some fields. They’re free for candidates.', 'Take breaks. Burning out mid-search helps nobody.'] },
    cv: { label: 'CV or résumé', tips: ['Keep it clear and short: usually one or two pages.', 'Put your most relevant experience first.', 'Use bullet points that show results: "cut waiting times by 20%" beats "responsible for queues".', 'Match the language to the job advert so the right skills stand out.', 'Start bullet points with action words: led, built, improved, organised.', 'Include skills you can actually talk about in an interview.', 'A short profile at the top can say who you are and what you want.', 'Check spelling and formatting carefully, or ask someone to read it.', 'Leave out things like your photo or date of birth unless they’re expected where you live.', 'Gaps are ok. A short honest line about what you did helps.', 'Save it as a PDF unless they ask for something else.', 'Keep a master CV with everything, then trim it for each job.'] },
    interview: { label: 'Interviews', tips: ['Research the organisation: what they do, who they serve, recent news.', 'Prepare examples using STAR: situation, task, action, result.', 'Practise out loud, even to a mirror or a friend.', 'Have two or three questions ready to ask them.', 'Plan your route or test your video call setup the day before.', 'It’s ok to pause and think before answering.', 'If you don’t know something, say how you’d find out.', 'Nerves are normal. Interviewers expect them.', 'Bring a copy of your CV and the job advert to glance at.', 'Thank them at the end, and maybe send a short thank-you email.', 'Talk about what you did, not just what the team did.', 'Every interview is practice, even the ones that don’t work out.'] },
    change: { label: 'Changing career', tips: ['Write down what you enjoy, what drains you, and what you’re good at. Patterns show up.', 'Talk to people already doing the job you’re curious about. Most are happy to chat.', 'Try it small first: a course, a side project, volunteering or shadowing.', 'Look for skills that carry over. You probably have more than you think.', 'It’s normal to take a small step back to move forward in a new field.', 'Plan your finances so the change isn’t a cliff edge.', 'Free careers services exist in many places and can help you map options.', 'A change doesn’t have to be all at once.', 'Update your CV to tell the story of why you’re moving.', 'It’s never too late to change direction.'] },
    pay: { label: 'Asking for a raise', tips: ['Research what similar roles pay in your area.', 'Keep a list of your achievements and the impact they had.', 'Pick a good moment, like a review, rather than a stressful day.', 'Ask clearly for a specific figure or range.', 'Focus on your value and results, not personal costs.', 'If the answer is no, ask what you’d need to do to get there, and by when.', 'Other things can be negotiated too: training, flexibility, title, holiday.', 'Practise the conversation beforehand.', 'Follow up in writing so there’s a record of what was agreed.', 'Asking is normal. Good employers expect it.'] },
    burnout: { label: 'Burnout', tips: ['Burnout is exhaustion, not laziness. Signs include dread, cynicism and feeling numb.', 'Talk to your manager or HR about workload if it’s safe to.', 'Protect your breaks and leave on time where you can.', 'Notice what drains you most and what gives you energy.', 'Use your holiday. It’s there for a reason.', 'A doctor can help, and may suggest time off if you need it.', 'Rest has to include things you enjoy, not just recovering to work again.', 'Lower the bar on non-essential things for a while.', 'Talk to people outside work about how you’re feeling.', 'If the job itself is the problem, it’s ok to start planning a change.'] },
    conflict: { label: 'Trouble at work', tips: ['Write down what happened, when, and who was there. Facts help later.', 'If you can, talk to the person directly and calmly first.', 'Use "I" statements: "I felt left out of the decision" rather than blame.', 'If it continues, talk to your manager or HR.', 'If you’re being bullied, harassed or discriminated against, that’s serious. Seek advice from HR, a union or an employment advice service.', 'Look up your workplace policies so you know your options.', 'Keep your own wellbeing in mind. Conflict is draining.', 'Find a trusted colleague or friend to talk it through.', 'Not every conflict needs a fight. Sometimes a boundary is enough.', 'Know your rights. Employment advice services can explain them for free in many places.'] },
    first: { label: 'Starting out', tips: ['Everyone starts somewhere. Entry roles are stepping stones.', 'Apprenticeships, internships and traineeships can be great ways in.', 'Part-time jobs build skills employers value: reliability, teamwork, communication.', 'Ask questions in your first weeks. People expect it.', 'Find someone experienced who’s happy to give you advice.', 'Keep a note of what you learn and achieve. It helps with your next CV.', 'Be on time, follow through, and be kind. It goes a long way.', 'It’s ok not to know what you want to do yet.', 'Your first job doesn’t decide your whole career.', 'Careers services at schools and colleges are free. Use them.'] },
    lost: { label: 'Lost my job', tips: ['Losing a job is a big loss. It’s ok to feel shaken, angry or sad.', 'Give yourself a few days to breathe before diving into applications.', 'Check what support or benefits you may be entitled to.', 'Look at your final pay, notice and any redundancy rights.', 'Tell your network you’re looking. People often want to help.', 'Keep a routine: get up, get dressed, plan your day.', 'Ask for a reference while things are fresh.', 'It’s not a reflection of your worth.', 'Talk to someone about how you’re feeling, not just the practical side.', 'Small steps count. One application today is progress.'] }
  },
  close: ['Good luck. You’ve got this.', 'Hope that helps a bit. Take it one step at a time.', 'Remember: general tips, so adapt them to where you live and what you do.', 'For advice tailored to you, a careers or employment advice service can help, often for free.', 'Back to the game whenever you’re ready.', 'Rooting for you, genuinely.']
}
});
